==Demographics==
{{Main|Demographics of Jatiel}}

{{See also|Demographics of Balisca}}Because of its status as an [[Aethiopian Ocean|Aethopic]] port, Jatiel has always been one of the main gateways into Balisca. This has attracted many immigrants and made Jatiel a cosmopolitan [[W:Melting pot|melting pot]]. By the end of the 18th century about half the population originated from elsewhere in [[Balisca]], and also from around [[Abyala]].

Economic conditions and political unrest in the [[Bayani Empire]] and the rest of the world brought several other waves of immigrants during the 20th century: [[TBA]] and [[TBA]] started arriving at the end of the 19th century and in the first half of the 20th century, up to 40% of the city's population was of Levantine origin; [[Kalinova|Kalinovans]] in 1917; [[Vasaras|Vasarasi]] in 1915 and 1923; "VIETNAMESE" in the 1920s, 1954 and after 1975; [[Echia|Echians]] during the 1920s and 1930s; Spaniards after 1936; [[Moro people|Moros]] (both [[Bayani people|Bayani]] and [[W:Berber people|Imfezzans]]) in the [[W:Interwar period|inter-war period]]; Sudanese Kaftians after 1952.

At the 2020 census, 38.8% of the inhabitants of the Jatiel metropolitan area were native-born Baliscans, 28.6% were naturalized citizens, and 32.6% were born in foreign countries. A quarter of the immigrants living in the Jatiel metropolitan area were born in the rest of Abyala (half of them in [[Asikyira]], [[Lacashe]], and [[Potocsí]]), 46% were born in [[West Kaftia]] (almost two-thirds of them in [[Ayorin]]), 14% in the rest of Kaftia (almost half of them from [[Dakare]], [[Tamazgha]] and [[Bonande]]), and 15.0% in the rest of the world.

In 2022, it was estimated that about one third of the population of Jatiel can trace their roots back to [[East Illypnia]] and [[West Fosia|Western Fosia]] (EIWF). Other significant migrant communities in Jatiel include [[Moro people|Moro]] (specifically  [[Bayani Baliscans|Bayani]] and [[Imfezzan Baliscans|Imfezzan]]), [[Andalus|Andalusians]], [[Jade Baliscans|Jade]], [[Sugihan Baliscans|Nusarayans]] and [[Dagit|Dagitis]].

In 2022, in several barrios, about 50% of the young people under 18 were of foreign origin (at least one immigrant parent).

===Sexual orientation and gender identity===
{{Main|LGBT demographics in Balisca}}
Jatiel has one of the most prominent LGBTQ communities within the Baliscan Confederation. The city has had a queer nightlife scene as early as the 1910s. Jatiel's [[Orquídeas]] barrio has a current status as a [[W:Gay mecca|queer village]] that attracts over 2 million LGBTQ visitors a year. 

With its spirit and vibrant urban atmosphere, Jatiel has become one of the top gay holiday spots for [[W:LGBTQ tourism|LGBTQ tourists]] and is at the centre of queer Abyalan nightlife, including spas, shopping, and dining.

